WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.295
Idea. A supermarket needs shelf stackers. The supermarket wants workers, and local people offer to work. They meet in the labour market, and agree a wage.

00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:24.697
Wage rate goes up the side, and quantity of workers along the bottom. Demand for labour slopes down. Supply of labour slopes up. They cross at the equilibrium wage, W e, and quantity, Q e.

00:00:25.280 --> 00:00:33.427
Changes in how many people can work move supply. More people available moves it right. Fewer people available moves it left.

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:45.077
Method. Step one, decide which curve moves. Step two, decide the direction. Step three, draw the new curve parallel to the old one, and label it S one.

00:00:45.640 --> 00:01:00.436
Step four, find where S one crosses demand. Step five, draw lines to both axes, and label the new wage, W one, and quantity, Q one. Here supply rose, so the wage falls and employment rises.

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:08.529
Learn the two results. Supply right: wage falls, workers rise. Supply left: wage rises, workers fall.

00:01:09.640 --> 00:01:24.944
Worked example. Here is a real question from June 2022. Draw the effects of an increase in the school-leaving age on the labour market. Label the new curve, the new wage rate and the new quantity of workers. Three marks.

00:01:25.520 --> 00:01:33.369
Young people must stay in education for longer. So fewer people are available to work, and the supply of labour shifts left.

00:01:33.920 --> 00:01:38.607
Draw S one parallel to S, on its left. Leave demand where it is.

00:01:39.160 --> 00:01:55.457
S one crosses demand higher up. Mark W one above W e, and Q one to the left of Q e. The wage rises and fewer workers are employed. This explanation is by olevels dot com, not an official mark scheme.

00:01:56.560 --> 00:02:06.020
Common mistake. A common mistake is shifting supply right for a higher school-leaving age. Fewer young people can work, so supply moves left.

00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:14.117
Another is moving demand, or forgetting the labels. Move supply, and label S one, W one and Q one.

00:02:15.240 --> 00:02:30.405
Your turn. Now you try. Many workers move into the country of Varia to work on building sites. On a labour market diagram for building workers, show the effect. Label the new curve, wage and quantity. Pause here and work it out.

00:02:31.520 --> 00:02:39.381
Answer. This is immigration. More people are available to work on building sites, so the supply of labour shifts right.

00:02:39.960 --> 00:02:52.779
Draw S one to the right of S. It crosses demand lower down. W one is below W e, and Q one is to the right of Q e. The wage falls and more workers are employed.

00:02:53.360 --> 00:03:02.879
Remember: which curve and which way, draw and label it, then mark W one and Q one at the new crossing. Now try the practice questions.
